I'd add one middleware in `app.js` before `sessionUser` (line 11) and write the log line when the response ends, not when the request arrives. Mounting it first means the 401s from `sessionUser` get logged too. `req.user` is already set by the time the callback runs, so the line can still include the user and account ids.

```js
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const start = process.hrtime.bigint();
  res.on("close", () => {
    console.log(JSON.stringify({
      method: req.method,
      path: req.originalUrl,
      status: res.writableFinished ? res.statusCode : "aborted",
      ms: Number(process.hrtime.bigint() - start) / 1e6,
      userId: req.user && req.user.id,
      accountId: req.user && req.user.accountId,
    }));
  });
  next();
});
```

Listening for `close` rather than `finish` also records requests that never got a response, such as the hanging unhandled rejections on Express 4.

Don't log request bodies or headers. They contain `billing_email` and the `session` cookie, and that cookie is a live credential.

